a question is drawn with its choices, and can be answered

§1.7, whose requirement for this head is stated in five words: **"A … cannot answer one
at all."** That is measured rather than relayed, and the measurement is the interesting
part because it is invisible in both the type and the screen:

- A **`question` carries `options: []`** and puts the model's offered answers in
  `choices` (`Vec<String>`) — the two kinds do not keep their rows in one field.
- Every row of this head's ladder machinery asked **`options.len()`**, so for a question
  the bound was zero: the arrows had nothing to wrap on, the digits had nothing to
  select, `answer_marked` returned `None` for the empty ladder, and a typed line was
  **held** with *"this ask offers no options — your line is held"*.
- And `OpenDecision::choices` was read by **nothing in this head** — checked by grep
  rather than assumed — so a question rendered as a headline over an empty ladder.

**Nothing was missing on the wire.** `ClientFrame::AnswerQuestion` carrying
`QuestionAnswer { option, note, free }` has existed since protocol 5, the client method
`answer_question` has existed with **no caller**, and the daemon validates the answer
against `choices` and refuses a permission/question mix-up by name. The whole gap was
this head's: no `Action` variant, no driver arm, no rendering, and a row count read
from the wrong field.

## The fix, and it is one function plus three callers

`decision_rows(d)` is **the one place the two kinds' row counts are reconciled** — a
permission counts `options`, a question counts `choices` — and the reason it is a
function rather than a condition is that four sites have to agree about it: the bound
the arrows wrap on, the bound the digits use, the row `answer_marked` takes, and the
rows the card draws. Those four disagreeing is exactly the defect above, and it is
silent: a wrong count does not throw.

Then D10's three fields, one at a time, which the requirement names as one vocabulary
rather than a menu plus a fallback:

- **By index** — arrows and Enter, and a digit, the same contract every other ladder in
  this file keeps. An answer of `choosing(i)`.
- **By the choice's own text** — typing what the card offered answers with that
  **index**, not with prose, so the model gets back *which of the three it offered*.
  Case and surrounding whitespace are a person's, not a spelling test.
- **Free text** — *"a typed answer, and a first-class one"*, and the sentence the
  requirement is named against is Claude Code's *"chat later"*, which turns a question
  into a suspended turn instead of an answer. A line that is not one of the choices is
  `free`, not a mistake to be corrected.

**Two frames, not one ladder.** `Answer` grants or denies a **permission** — failure
mode *something runs*. `AnswerQuestion` answers *which way should I go* — failure mode
*a person is quoted as saying something they did not*. `question.rs` already argues
this at length and `hub.rs` enforces it: a permission's answer sent to a question is
refused by name, and the check is written fail-closed (only a request that *says* it is
a question accepts a person's sentence). So the two kinds keep their own frames, and
this commit adds the sender the second one never had.

## The boundary the four sites agree about

**A question whose model offered nothing is still answerable in words.** No rows means
no marked answer and no digits — `answer_marked` returns `None` — and the composer
still answers. And an **empty** line on such a question answers nothing at all rather
than sending an empty answer: the daemon refuses `QuestionAnswer::default()` as
`AnswerDefect::Empty`, and a refusal the head could have predicted is a keystroke
thrown away. Both are asserted.

## The card

The rows come from whichever field the kind keeps them in, which is the rendering half
of the same bug. A question's label is the **choice itself**, with no `(option_id)`
beside it — a question's answers have no ids; they are the model's prose offered back
as an index — and its hint is its own: *"↑↓ to choose · Enter to answer · or type your
own answer"*, because *"or type the id"* names something a question has not got, and the
free field is the point.

## Tests

- `a_question_is_drawn_with_its_choices_and_can_be_answered_three_ways` — the card
  draws the choices and no longer claims the ask offered none; index by arrows and
  Enter; index by the choice's text, including a person's casing (`  SQLITE  `); free
  text; and the no-choices boundary in both directions (words work, empty does not).
  **One of my own assertions was wrong and the failure is worth recording**: I wrote
  `PostgreSQL` as the case-fold example for the choice `postgres`, and it is ten
  characters against eight — a *different word*, so it correctly fell through to free
  text. The test now asserts both: `  SQLITE  ` is the choice, `PostgreSQL` is words.
- `the_row_count_is_the_kinds_own_field_in_both_directions` — `decision_rows` on each
  kind with the other's field asserted empty, and the answers going through the two
  different `Action`s. A permission read as a question would answer `option: None` with
  an option's label as free text, which is a grant nobody gave.

`cargo test -p letibot-tui`: **306 passed, 0 failed, 4 ignored** (304 + 2).

**Not verified, and said rather than glossed:** `rustfmt --check` still cannot run — it
writes a temp file and `bash` is closed for that direction by the circuit breaker, which
only the operator lifts. `letibot-harnessd`, `-sessionlog`, `-tokencore` and `-turn`
have not been re-run since `65725ce`; that run was started twice and contended on the
cargo lock with my own builds both times, so it is not that they fail — it is that I do
not have their result. Nothing in this commit or that one touches them directly, and
`clauses.rs`, where the only dependency on the removed refusal actually lived, is green.

**Latent in both heads, and live the day something raises one**: `RequestKind::Question`
is **never constructed** anywhere in this tree — every site is
`RequestKind::Permission` — so this path has no producer yet. That is why the
requirement is *correctness, not urgency*, and it is also why it is worth fixing now:
the two heads disagreeing about a frame nobody sends is exactly the state that makes
the first sender a bug report.

## Not in this commit

**`note`** — D10's middle field, *"option B, but only for the CUDA box"* — is carried by
the wire and by `QuestionAnswer`, and **no head can set it**. The requirement says so
for both: *"Neither can send `note` or `free`"* was true of both before this; `free` is
true no longer, and `note` still is. It needs a syntax (`2 — only for the CUDA box`?) and
that is a decision about a card's grammar rather than a missing line of code, so it is
recorded here rather than half-invented.
